(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-viral-and-fungal-infections-vignettes"]={"title":"Cutaneous Viral and Fungal Infections Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient with forehead zoster has no lesions on the nose. What does that tell you about his risk of eye involvement?","choices":["Absence of the Hutchinson sign reliably excludes eye involvement","Absence of the Hutchinson sign means ophthalmology review can wait two weeks","Absence of the Hutchinson sign confirms involvement of a different cranial nerve","Absence of the Hutchinson sign does not exclude eye involvement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a negative sign is not reassuring. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 4-year-old has about fifteen discrete, smooth, firm, flesh-coloured dome-shaped pearly papules on the trunk, each 3 to 5 millimetres with a dimple in the centre. They are asymptomatic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sebaceous hyperplasia","Molluscum contagiosum","Verruca vulgaris","Varicella"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — central umbilication is characteristic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"A patient with varicella has severe, disseminated disease. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Intravenous acyclovir","Oral acyclovir alone at standard dose","Supportive care alone with no antiviral","Varicella-zoster immune globulin alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reserved for severe or disseminated disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which self-care measure does the lecture single out as essential for a patient with tinea pedis?","choices":["Wearing occlusive footwear throughout the day","Soaking the feet in hot water each evening","Applying a corticosteroid powder to the shoes","Drying between the toes after bathing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck flags this one as essential. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A patient with recurrent genital herpes simplex asks how to reduce the risk of infecting his partner. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Neither suppressive antivirals nor condoms affect transmission at all, so the only option available is to avoid sexual contact permanently","Suppressive valacyclovir lowers transmission and condoms reduce risk, but neither eliminates it; avoid contact during the prodrome and while lesions are active","Suppressive valacyclovir eliminates transmission entirely, so no other precaution is needed once it is started","Condoms eliminate transmission entirely, so antiviral therapy adds nothing for a patient who uses them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — risk is reduced, never abolished. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with candidal intertrigo is prescribed a topical azole. Beyond the antifungal, which environmental measures does the lecture require?","choices":["Expose the folds to direct sunlight daily, avoid moisture-wicking fabric, and treat incontinence only if it is severe","Gently dry the folds, reduce friction and occlusion, use moisture-wicking fabric or absorbent material, and address incontinence or hyperhidrosis","Apply an occlusive dressing to the folds, avoid all washing and drying of the area, use a non-absorbent fabric, and treat incontinence only once the rash has settled","Soak the folds in warm water twice daily, apply talcum powder, and cover the area with a waterproof film"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — correcting the environment is listed first. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A child has breakthrough varicella with lesions that do not form crusts. How is the end of the contagious period determined?","choices":["When 21 days have elapsed since the exposure","When the fever has been absent for 24 hours","When no new lesions have appeared for 24 hours","When the last lesion has been present for 24 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the rule for breakthrough disease without crusts. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A patient asks whether cryotherapy will cure her human papillomavirus infection. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Only surgical excision eradicates the virus, so it should be arranged now","No therapy eradicates the virus with certainty, and recurrence can occur","Cryotherapy eradicates the virus reliably, so recurrence will not occur","Salicylic acid eradicates the virus reliably, though cryotherapy does not"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — set expectations before starting treatment. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"A patient has limited onychomycosis affecting one nail and wishes to avoid oral therapy. Which options does the lecture give, and with what caveat?","choices":["Topical nystatin, terbinafine cream, or selenium sulfide, with equal cure rates","Topical efinaconazole, tavaborole, or ciclopirox, with higher cure rates than oral therapy","No topical option exists, so oral therapy is the only route for any nail disease","Topical efinaconazole, tavaborole, or ciclopirox, with lower cure rates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an honest trade-off to explain to the patient. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 70-year-old presents 36 hours after the onset of a thoracic zoster rash. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Observe without treatment, since the rash will resolve on its own","Start oral valacyclovir, famciclovir, or acyclovir as soon as possible","Start a topical antiviral applied to the affected dermatome","Start an oral corticosteroid alone without any antiviral"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ideally within 72 hours of rash onset. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 23-year-old has finely scaling tan macules on the trunk, and you perform potassium hydroxide microscopy on a scale from one lesion. Which finding would you expect?","choices":["Short hyphae with clusters of yeast, described as \"spaghetti and meatballs\"","Long branching septate hyphae without any yeast forms present","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae without any short hyphal fragments","Multinucleated giant cells with intranuclear inclusions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that is the classic appearance. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 64-year-old has thickened discoloured toenails and you decide to confirm the diagnosis before prescribing. Which tests does the lecture list, and how should the sample be taken?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide microscopy or fungal culture only; sample the most distal free edge of the nail plate before any trimming of the onycholytic nail is performed","Serology for dermatophyte antibodies or a blood culture; no local sampling of the nail is required at any stage","A Wood lamp examination followed by biopsy of the nail matrix; sample only after six weeks of topical therapy has failed","Potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid–Schiff stain of clippings, culture, or amplification testing; sample the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sample subungual debris after trimming the onycholytic nail. Sampling proximally is the part most often missed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A patient has a single 3-centimetre annular plaque on the trunk. Which is the most appropriate treatment, and how should it be applied?","choices":["Topical terbinafine, butenafine, or an azole, applied only to the cleared centre of the lesion","An oral antifungal from the outset, applied in every patient with a single localized plaque","A combination corticosteroid and antifungal product, applied to the lesion and well beyond its border","Topical terbinafine, butenafine, or an azole, applied to the lesion and 1 to 2 centimetres beyond its border"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — treating beyond the border covers the advancing edge. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A patient with tinea incognito has extensive, follicular disease. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Systemic antifungal therapy","Topical antifungal therapy alone","A more potent topical corticosteroid","Oral antibiotic therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — extensive, follicular or refractory infection warrants systemic therapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 59-year-old has right-sided facial droop, ear pain, and painful vesicles in the right external auditory canal. She also reports tinnitus and vertigo. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Herpes zoster ophthalmicus","Postherpetic neuralgia","Herpetic whitlow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — peripheral facial palsy with vesicles of the ear canal or auricle. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A healthy 55-year-old asks about the shingles vaccine. What should she be offered?","choices":["Two doses of recombinant zoster vaccine, but only from the age of 65","No vaccine, since she is under 65 and immunocompetent","Two doses of recombinant zoster vaccine, at a standard interval of 2 to 6 months","One dose of recombinant zoster vaccine, with no second dose required"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — two doses for immunocompetent adults 50 and over. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A parent asks when her child with varicella may return to normal activities. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["The child is contagious for 21 days from exposure regardless of the lesions","The child is contagious only while new vesicles are forming, not once any have crusted","The child is contagious from 1 to 2 days before the rash until all lesions have crusted","The child is contagious only from the day the rash appears until the fever resolves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — contagiousness begins before the rash is visible. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A colleague suggests incising and draining the swollen fingertip of a patient with herpetic whitlow. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Incise and drain it, but only after 72 hours of oral antiviral therapy have failed","Do not incise and drain it, because the lesion is sterile and will resolve within 24 hours","Do not incise and drain it, because that does not treat the virus and can delay healing","Incise and drain it, because decompression shortens the course of the infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — explicitly contraindicated. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"You are about to prescribe a systemic antifungal for an 8-year-old with confirmed tinea capitis. Which safety step does the lecture identify before the first dose?","choices":["Review drug interactions and hepatic disease, and obtain baseline liver tests when indicated by the agent, its labeling, and patient risk","Review drug interactions and renal disease, and obtain a baseline creatinine in every patient regardless of the agent chosen","Review drug interactions and cardiac disease, and obtain a baseline electrocardiogram in every patient before the first dose","No safety step is needed, because oral antifungals used for scalp disease carry no clinically important interactions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — baseline testing is indication-driven, not automatic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 63-year-old has a grouped vesicular eruption that is not clearly dermatomal. Which conditions does the lecture list in the differential diagnosis of herpes zoster?","choices":["Basal cell carcinoma, sebaceous hyperplasia, and condyloma acuminatum","Folliculitis, psoriasis, seborrheic dermatitis, and alopecia areata","Herpes simplex, contact dermatitis, impetigo, folliculitis, insect bites, and varicella","Psoriasis, nummular eczema, atopic dermatitis, discoid lupus, and fixed drug eruption"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — dermatitis herpetiformis is on the list too, making seven named entities. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl has molluscum lesions in the genital area. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["No assessment at all, because genital molluscum is expected in young children","Screening for sexually transmitted infections in every case as the sole action","Context-sensitive assessment, because location alone does not prove abuse","Immediate mandatory reporting on the basis of the lesion location alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck is deliberate about this. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 5-year-old with varicella has fever and discomfort. The mother asks which over-the-counter medicine to give. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Give an oral antiviral to every child, and avoid all analgesia until the lesions have crusted","Give a systemic corticosteroid for the inflammation, and avoid antihistamines for the itch","Give supportive care, avoid aspirin, and use caution with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Give supportive care, avoid paracetamol, and use aspirin freely for the fever and discomfort"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — aspirin is avoided in children with varicella. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 78-year-old had a thoracic zoster rash four months ago. The rash healed, but she has constant burning pain in the same area and cannot bear her shirt touching the skin. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Postherpetic neuralgia","Zoster sine herpete","Recurrent herpes zoster in the same dermatome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pain persisting 90 days or more after rash onset, with allodynia. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A patient with acute zoster has mild pain. Which comfort measures does the lecture describe?","choices":["No analgesia at all, with cool compresses, calamine, loose clothing, and lesion coverage","Acetaminophen or a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug, with cool compresses, calamine, loose clothing, and lesion coverage","A long-acting opioid from the outset, with warm compresses, occlusive dressings, tight clothing, and the lesions left uncovered","A systemic corticosteroid from the outset, with cool compresses, calamine, and lesion coverage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — lesion coverage also reduces exposure of contacts. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A patient has confirmed dermatophyte onychomycosis of the toenails. Which is the first-line treatment, and for how long?","choices":["Oral terbinafine, usually for 12 weeks","Oral terbinafine, usually for 6 weeks","Topical ciclopirox, usually for 12 weeks","Oral fluconazole, usually for 12 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — fingernails are usually 6 weeks, toenails 12. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient has interdigital tinea pedis limited to two web spaces. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral terbinafine for twelve weeks from the outset","Topical nystatin applied to both feet twice daily","An oral antibiotic plus a topical corticosteroid","Topical terbinafine, butenafine, or an azole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — localized disease is usually treated topically. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A Wood lamp examination is performed on a child with suspected tinea capitis in the United States and shows no fluorescence. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["It does not exclude the diagnosis, because Trichophyton tonsurans usually does not fluoresce","It excludes the diagnosis, because all dermatophytes causing scalp disease fluoresce","It excludes the diagnosis, because a negative lamp is more sensitive than culture","It confirms a Microsporum infection, because that species characteristically fails to fluoresce"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and T. tonsurans is the commonest species in the United States. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 22-year-old with pityriasis versicolor is prescribed selenium sulfide and asks exactly how to use it. Which regimen does the lecture describe?","choices":["Twice-daily application for 6 weeks, rinsed off after one minute","Daily application for 7 days, with a 10-minute contact time before rinsing","Daily application for 7 days, rinsed off immediately after application","Weekly application for 7 weeks, with a 10-minute contact time before rinsing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the contact time matters as much as the frequency. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 55-year-old labourer has a thickened, dry, scaly right palm. He assumed it was from hard work. His left foot has interdigital scaling. Which term describes this pattern?","choices":["Id reaction, in which a dermatitis appears at a site distant from the primary infection","Tinea incognito, in which topical steroids alter the clinical appearance","Mosaic pattern, in which lesions cluster together on the weight-bearing surface","Two feet–one hand syndrome, in which the hand used to scratch the foot becomes affected"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and patients often blame dry skin or manual labour. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 26-year-old develops an intensely itchy papulovesicular eruption on the fingers about ten days after being treated for a foot infection. Careful examination shows an asymptomatic fissure and maceration in the toe webs. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A dermatophytid (id) reaction","Tinea manuum","Vesiculobullous tinea pedis","Herpetic whitlow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a distant inflammatory dermatitis, one to two weeks after the primary infection. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 60"}]};
